        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "## 2 Definitions and Preliminaries",
            "block_bbox": [
                161,
                270,
                724,
                305
            ],
            "block_id": 1,
            "block_order": 2,
            "group_id": 1,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    161.0,
                    270.0
                ],
                [
                    724.0,
                    270.0
                ],
                [
                    724.0,
                    305.0
                ],
                [
                    161.0,
                    305.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "All graphs in this paper are assumed to be finite, simple, and connected. We follow standard notation from [9] for concepts not defined here. The incidence matrix B of a graph  $ \\Gamma $ is the  $ \\{0,1\\} $-matrix with rows indexed by vertices and columns indexed by edges, such that the  $ (i,j) $-entry equals 1 if and only if vertex  $ i $ is an endpoint of edge  $ j $. If  $ \\Gamma $ has  $ n $ vertices and  $ e $ edges, then  $ B $ has order  $ n \\times e $.",
            "block_bbox": [
                160,
                330,
                1022,
                475
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 2,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    160.0,
                    330.0
                ],
                [
                    1022.0,
                    330.0
                ],
                [
                    1022.0,
                    475.0
                ],
                [
                    160.0,
                    475.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The rank of the incidence matrix has a clean combinatorial description: if  $ \\Gamma $ is a graph with  $ n $ vertices and  $ c_0 $ bipartite connected components, then  $ \\text{rank}(B) = n - c_0 $. In particular, if  $ \\Gamma $ is a connected bipartite graph with  $ n $ vertices, then  $ \\text{rank}(B) = n - 1 $.",
            "block_bbox": [
                160,
                475,
                1018,
                588
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    160.0,
                    475.0
                ],
                [
                    1018.0,
                    475.0
                ],
                [
                    1018.0,
                    588.0
                ],
                [
                    160.0,
                    588.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The line graph of a graph  $ \\Gamma $ is the graph  $ L(\\Gamma) $ whose vertex set is  $ E(\\Gamma) $, with two edges of  $ \\Gamma $ being adjacent in  $ L(\\Gamma) $ if and only if they share an endpoint in  $ \\Gamma $.",
            "block_bbox": [
                160,
                590,
                1018,
                649
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    160.0,
                    590.0
                ],
                [
                    1018.0,
                    590.0
                ],
                [
                    1018.0,
                    649.0
                ],
                [
                    160.0,
                    649.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Lemma 2.1 ([9]) Let $B$ be the incidence matrix of the graph $\\Gamma$ on $n$ vertices and $m$ edges, and let $L$ be the line graph of $\\Gamma$. Then $B^{T}B = A(L) + 2I_{m}$, where $A(L)$ is the adjacency matrix of the line graph of $\\Gamma$.",
            "block_bbox": [
                160,
                671,
                1022,
                759
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    160.0,
                    671.0
                ],
                [
                    1022.0,
                    671.0
                ],
                [
                    1022.0,
                    759.0
                ],
                [
                    160.0,
                    759.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Lemma 2.2 ( [9]) Let $B$ be the incidence matrix of the graph $\\Gamma$ on $n$ vertices and $m$ edges. Then $BB^{T} = \\Delta(\\Gamma) + A(\\Gamma)$, where $\\Delta(\\Gamma)$ is the diagonal $n \\times n$ matrix whose $ii$-entry is equal to the degree of vertex $i$.",
            "block_bbox": [
                160,
                781,
                1023,
                869
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    160.0,
                    781.0
                ],
                [
                    1023.0,
                    781.0
                ],
                [
                    1023.0,
